 "blocks": [
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_yQxMM5BQ4NjtH9mF",
   "name": "Derivation of KE = 1/2mv2",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Derivation of KE = 1/2mv<sup>2</sup>"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Kinetic energy is energy an object has due to its **motion** (or velocity)\n- Resultant forces make objects accelerate as described by Newton's second law *F = ma*\n- Work is done by that force and energy is transferred to the object\n- Using this concept of work done and an equation of motion, the extra work done due to an object's speed can be derived"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Consider a mass, *m*, at rest which accelerates to a velocity, *v*, over a displacement, *s*\n- The work done in accelerating the mass is:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$W=Fs$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Newton's Second Law states that:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$F=ma$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The suvat equation for velocity is: $v^{2}=u^{2}+2as$\n\n  - If the initial velocity is zero, *u* = 0\n  - And displacement, *s*\n  - Then: $v^{2}=2as$\n  - Rearranging to make *a* the subject:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$a=\\frac{v^{2}}{2s}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Substituting this expression for *a* into Newton's Second Law gives:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$F=ma=\\frac{mv^{2}}{2s}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Substituting this expression for *F* into the work done equation gives:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$W=\\frac{mv^{2}}{2s}\\timess=\\frac{1}{2}mv^{2}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The mass is now able to do extra work due to its speed\n\n  - The amount of extra work is equal to $\\frac{1}{2}mv^{2}$\n- The mass now has:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$E_{k}=\\frac{1}{2}mv^{2}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_RSSY9GnfpxwM3HZp",
   "name": "Kinetic Energy",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Kinetic energy"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The **faster **an object is moving, the **greater **its **kinetic energy**\n- When an object is falling, it is **gaining** kinetic energy since it is gaining speed\n- This energy transferred from the **gravitational potential energy** it is losing\n- An object will maintain this kinetic energy unless its speed changes"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The amount of kinetic energy an object has is determined by its mass and its speed:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$E_{k}=\\frac{1}{2}mv^{2}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Where:\n\n  - *E*<sub>k</sub> = kinetic energy in joules (J)\n  - *m* = mass in kilograms (kg)\n  - *v* = velocity in metres per second (m s<sup>-1</sup>)"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "A car travelling forwards"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/09/5.2.4-Kinetic-energy-diagram.png",
   "file": "assets/5d772b3359f0-5-2-4-kinetic-energy-diagram.png",
   "alt": "Kinetic energy diagram, downloadable AS & A Level Physics revision notes",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "***Kinetic energy is the energy an object has when it is moving, determined by its mass and its speed***"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "A body travelling with a speed of 12 m s<sup>-1</sup> has kinetic energy 1650 J. The speed of the body is increased to 45 m s<sup>-1</sup>. Determine the body's new kinetic energy.\n\n**Answer:**\n\n**Step 1: List the known quantities**\n\n- Initial speed, *v*<sub>i</sub> = 12 m s<sup>-1</sup>\n- Initial kinetic energy, *E*<sub>k i</sub> = 1650 J\n- Final speed, *v*<sub>f</sub> = 45 m s<sup>-1</sup>\n\n**Step 2: State the kinetic energy equation**\n\n$E_{k}=\\frac{1}{2}mv^{2}$\n\n**Step 3: Determine the object's mass**\n\n- The mass will not change\n- Therefore, mass can be calculated from the initial kinetic energy\n\n$m=\\frac{2E_{ki}}{v_{i}^{2}}$\n\n$m=\\frac{2\\times1650}{12^{2}}$\n\n$m=23\\mathrm{kg}$\n\n**Step 4: Substitute the known values to calculate the final kinetic energy**\n\n$E_{kf}=\\frac{1}{2}mv_{f}^{2}$\n\n$E_{kf}=0.5\\times23\\times45^{2}$\n\n$E_{kf}=23000J$"
  }
 ]
}